N
ACTIVE EXPLOITATION

Aliases: OAuth auto-approval to agent-memory authority; vault-token
bootstrap; unauthenticated persistent-context poisoning.

Canonical fingerprint: network-reachable MCP OAuth endpoint ->
authorization without user authentication/consent -> static vault
token -> full MCP vault authority -> persistent knowledge/context
modification -> later agent consumption -> privileged downstream
action UNKNOWN.

Fresh evidence: CVE-2026-54618, published 17 Sep 2026, affects
Obsidian Web MCP before 0.2.0. Public advisory data states
/oauth/authorize could issue an authorization code without login,
consent, or session validation; /oauth/token could exchange it for the
static VAULT_MCP_TOKEN without authenticating a client. A reachable
caller could then invoke vault_read, vault_write, vault_search,
vault_list, vault_move and vault_delete across the vault. Optional
PKCE did not stop attacker-initiated authorization. Version 0.2.0
fixes the issue. No public active exploitation is established.

Stages/edges:

ENTRY network-reachable OAuth/MCP service [CONFIRMED/T1]

-> authorization code without authenticated resource owner [CONFIRMED/T1]

-> static VAULT_MCP_TOKEN acquisition [CONFIRMED/T1]

-> MCP vault authority [CONFIRMED/T1]

-> vault read/write/search/delete [CONFIRMED capability/T1]

-> persistent modification of agent-consumed knowledge [INFERRED:
capability established, later agent consumption deployment-dependent]

-> later agent behavior influenced [UNKNOWN]

-> external privileged tool/cloud/signer action [UNKNOWN].

Prerequisites: vulnerable version; OAuth/MCP deployment reachable to
caller. Trust boundaries: unauthenticated network -> OAuth
authorization server; OAuth token -> MCP resource authority;
persistent vault -> future agent context. Authority transition: zero
user authority -> full static vault-token authority.

Persistence: vault writes can survive the originating request and
therefore create persistent state; persistence into a future agent
context is INFERRED unless that deployment actually consumes the
modified vault.

Propagation: UNKNOWN.

Strongest evidence: public CVE/GitHub-derived advisory description for
CVE-2026-54618.

Strongest counterevidence: no public exploit or in-the-wild
exploitation identified; no documented downstream agent action from a
poisoned vault.

2 MATERIAL CHAIN UPDATES

None accepted. Latest Incident Intake at 06:00 reported NO MATERIAL
DELTA. Latest Scanner at 05:49 reported 26 new scanner edges, but the
new accepted chain here is based on a newly published MCP OAuth
boundary failure rather than treating scanner findings as incident
confirmation.

3 TOP RECURRING SECOND-ORDER MECHANISMS

A. Authority bootstrap: authentication/identity boundary failure
yields a high-authority reusable capability.

B. Persistent-context authority: write access to agent-consumed state
can outlive the initiating session.

C. Identity-to-authority binding failure: nominal OAuth flow exists,
but resource-owner authentication/consent is not actually bound to
issuance.

D. Composition gap: vault write authority plus later agent trust may
compose into behavior authority even though neither grant explicitly
authorizes that composition.

4 STAGE/EDGE GRAPH

unauth network -> OAuth code [C/T1] -> static token [C/T1] -> vault
authority [C/T1] -> persistent vault mutation [C capability/T1] ->
future agent consumes mutation [I] -> privileged external action [U]

5 UNKNOWN LINKS WORTH RESOLVING

Highest-value edge: persistent agent knowledge mutation -> later
tool-authorized external action. Also unresolved: whether any public
deployment was exploited before patching; whether modified Obsidian
content is automatically elevated into trusted instructions by common
agent integrations.

6 DEFENSIVE CHOKEPOINTS + DETECTION

Require authenticated resource-owner session and explicit consent
before authorization-code issuance; bind codes to client and redirect
identity; avoid static bearer tokens representing an entire knowledge
store; scope read/write/delete separately; detect token issuance
without preceding authenticated session; alert on bulk or unusual
vault writes/deletes; provenance-tag externally mutable context; treat
persistent knowledge as untrusted data when re-entering an agent;
require separate authority for context mutation -> tool action
composition.

QP implication: ALLOW(write persistent context S) must not imply
ALLOW(S -> future privileged action). Authorization should bind issuer
identity, state provenance and downstream capability separately.

7 SAFE LAB REPRODUCTION QUEUE

Disposable local OAuth/MCP mock, dummy vault, fake static token,
harmless marker note. Verify that unauthenticated authorization cannot
produce a token and that a marker written by an untrusted identity
remains labelled untrusted when a dummy agent later reads it. No
production systems or real credentials.

8 CROSS-CASE CLUSTERS

Joins SHARED-CACHE-AUTHORITY on identity separation failure;
AGENT-PERSISTED-INSTRUCTION and AGENT-STIGMERGY-AUTHORITY-CASCADE on
persistent state becoming future agent input;
PLUGIN-PIN-IDENTITY-CONFUSION on authorization referring to an
identity/object that is not sufficiently bound to the authority
actually exercised.

